{"id":"neet-2026-q014","pn":14,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Resultant force and acceleration","q":"The magnitude and direction of the acceleration produced in a body of mass 5 kg when two mutually perpendicular forces 8 N and 6 N act on it, are respectively:","o":["2 m s⁻²; tan⁻¹(3/4) with 6 N force","2 m s⁻²; tan⁻¹(4/3) with 8 N force","2 m s⁻²; tan⁻¹(3/4) with 8 N force","20 m s⁻²; tan⁻¹(4/3) with 8 N force"],"c":2,"s":"F_net = √(6² + 8²) = 10 N.\na = F_net/m = 10/5 = 2 m/s².\ntanθ = 6/8.\nθ = tan⁻¹(3/4), measured from the 8 N force.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":125,"alt":"Forces 6 N up and 8 N right with resultant F_net at angle theta from the 8 N force","d":[["l",50,100,50,15,"a"],["l",50,100,150,100,"a"],["l",50,100,125,45,"a"],["t",20,20,"6 N"],["t",130,116,"8 N","m"],["t",130,44,"F_net","s"],["p","M72 100A22 22 0 0 0 67.7 86.9"],["t",76,95,"θ","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q019","pn":19,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Banking of roads","q":"A car travels on a circular racetrack of radius 50 m, which is banked at an angle θ. If the car travels at a speed 10 ms⁻¹, then the wear and tear on its tyres is minimum. Taking the acceleration due to gravity to be 10 ms⁻², the value of θ is:","o":["tan⁻¹(2/5)","tan⁻¹(√3/2)","tan⁻¹(2√3)","tan⁻¹(1/5)"],"c":3,"s":"Wear and tear is minimum when no friction is needed, so tan θ = v²/(rg).\ntan θ = (10 × 10)/(50 × 10) = 1/5.\nθ = tan⁻¹(1/5)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q035","pn":35,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Friction","q":"A box of mass 15 kg is kept on the floor of a stationary trolley. The coefficient of static friction between the box and the trolley is 0.12. Keeping the box in stationary state over the trolley, the maximum acceleration with which the trolley can be moved horizontally in m s⁻² is:\n(g = 10 m/s²)","o":["1.5","1.8","2.1","1.2"],"c":3,"s":"The box moves with the trolley only if the friction force can supply the needed force.\nMaximum friction μmg is balanced by ma.\nSo a = μg = 0.12 × 10 = 1.2 m/s².","sfig":{"w":150,"h":90,"alt":"A box on top of a trolley; an arrow labelled μmg points left and an arrow labelled ma points right at the box","d":[["r",17,27,101,46],["r",53,8,27,19],["l",53,17,38,17,"a"],["l",80,17,95,17,"a"],["t",4,21,"μmg","s"],["t",98,21,"ma","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q004","pn":4,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Friction on an inclined plane","q":"There are two inclined surfaces of equal length (L) and same angle of inclination 45° with the horizontal. One of them is rough and the other is perfectly smooth. A given body takes 2 times as much time to slide down on rough surface than on the smooth surface. The coefficient of kinetic friction (μ_k) between the object and the rough surface is close to","o":["0.25","0.40","0.5","0.75"],"c":3,"s":"t_rough = 2 t_smooth\na_smooth = g sinθ\nt ∝ 1/√a, so t_smooth ∝ 1/√(g sinθ)\na_rough = g sinθ − μ_k g cosθ\nt_rough/t_smooth = √(sinθ) / √(sinθ − μ_k cosθ) = 2\nSquaring both sides: sinθ/(sinθ − μ_k cosθ) = 4\n⇒ (1/√2) / (1/√2 − μ_k × 1/√2) = 4\n⇒ 1 − μ_k = 1/4\nμ_k = 3/4 = 0.75"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q010","pn":10,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Impulse and momentum","q":"A ball of mass 0.5 kg is dropped from a height of 40 m. The ball hits the ground and rises to a height of 10 m. The impulse imparted to the ball during its collision with the ground is (Take g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["21 NS","7 NS","0","84 NS"],"c":0,"s":"Speed just before hitting: v₁ = √(2gh₁) = √(2 × 9.8 × 40) = √784 = 28 m s⁻¹\nSpeed just after: v₂ = √(2gh₂) = √(2 × 9.8 × 10) = √196 = 14 m s⁻¹\nImpulse = Δp⃗ = m(v⃗_f − v⃗_i) = m(v⃗₂ − v⃗₁)\n= (1/2)(14 − (−28))\n= 21 NS","sfig":{"w":380,"h":190,"alt":"A ball dropped 40 m (dotted line) reaches the ground with velocity v1 downward, then rises with velocity v2 upward to 10 m; the ground is hatched.","d":[["l",96.3,7.2,96.3,155.7,"b"],["t",48.6,76.5,"40 m",""],["c",160.2,9,2.7,"F"],["l",160.2,9,160.2,153,"d"],["l",160.2,117,160.2,155.7,"a"],["t",135,137.7,"v₁",""],["c",186.3,67.5,2.7,"F"],["l",186.3,67.5,186.3,153,"d"],["l",186.3,154.8,186.3,114.3,"a"],["t",201.6,128.7,"v₂",""],["l",252.9,155.7,252.9,65.7,"b"],["t",262.8,114.3,"10 m",""],["l",110.7,157.5,324,157.5,""],["l",110.7,163.8,117,157.5,""],["l",117.9,163.8,124.2,157.5,""],["l",125.1,163.8,131.4,157.5,""],["l",132.3,163.8,138.6,157.5,""],["l",139.5,163.8,145.8,157.5,""],["l",146.7,163.8,153,157.5,""],["l",153.9,163.8,160.2,157.5,""],["l",161.1,163.8,167.4,157.5,""],["l",168.3,163.8,174.6,157.5,""],["l",175.5,163.8,181.8,157.5,""],["l",182.7,163.8,189,157.5,""],["l",189.9,163.8,196.2,157.5,""],["l",197.1,163.8,203.4,157.5,""],["l",204.3,163.8,210.6,157.5,""],["l",211.5,163.8,217.8,157.5,""],["l",218.7,163.8,225,157.5,""],["l",225.9,163.8,232.2,157.5,""],["l",233.1,163.8,239.4,157.5,""],["l",240.3,163.8,246.6,157.5,""],["l",247.5,163.8,253.8,157.5,""],["l",254.7,163.8,261,157.5,""],["l",261.9,163.8,268.2,157.5,""],["l",269.1,163.8,275.4,157.5,""],["l",276.3,163.8,282.6,157.5,""],["l",283.5,163.8,289.8,157.5,""],["l",290.7,163.8,297,157.5,""],["l",297.9,163.8,304.2,157.5,""],["l",305.1,163.8,311.4,157.5,""],["l",312.3,163.8,318.6,157.5,""],["l",319.5,163.8,325.8,157.5,""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Contact force between two blocks","q":"A horizontal force 10 N is applied to a block A as shown in figure. The mass of blocks A and B are 2 kg and 3 kg, respectively. The blocks slide over a frictionless surface. The force exerted by block A on block B is-","o":["6 N","10 N","zero","4 N"],"c":0,"s":"Treat A and B as one system: F = (M₁ + M₂)a.\na = 10/(2 + 3) = 2 m s⁻².\nForce of A on B, F′ = M₂a = 3 × 2 = 6 N.","fig":{"w":250,"h":95,"alt":"A horizontal force F = 10 N pushes block A (2 kg), which touches block B (3 kg), on a frictionless horizontal surface.","d":[["t",10,52,"F = 10 N"],["l",10,66,128,66,"a"],["r",130,50,42,30],["r",172,28,42,52],["t",151,61,"A","m"],["t",151,74,"2 kg","ms"],["t",193,46,"B","m"],["t",193,60,"3 kg","m"],["l",100,80,230,80],["l",104,80,99,86],["l",110,80,105,86],["l",116,80,111,86],["l",122,80,117,86],["l",128,80,123,86],["l",134,80,129,86],["l",140,80,135,86],["l",146,80,141,86],["l",152,80,147,86],["l",158,80,153,86],["l",164,80,159,86],["l",170,80,165,86],["l",176,80,171,86],["l",182,80,177,86],["l",188,80,183,86],["l",194,80,189,86],["l",200,80,195,86],["l",206,80,201,86],["l",212,80,207,86],["l",218,80,213,86],["l",224,80,219,86],["l",230,80,225,86]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q034","pn":34,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Circular motion tension","q":"A bob is whirled in a horizontal plane by means of a string with an initial speed of ω rpm. The tension in the string is T. If speed becomes 2ω while keeping the same radius, the tension in the string becomes;","o":["T/4","√2 T","T","4T"],"c":3,"s":"Tension gives the centripetal force: T = mℓω².\nWith 2ω: T′ = mℓ(2ω)² = 4mℓω².\nSo T′ = 4T.","sfig":{"w":140,"h":150,"alt":"Two sketches of a bob of mass m on a string of length l rotating about a fixed point, with angular speed omega and then 2 omega, tension T pointing towards the centre.","d":[["c",16,40,2,"F"],["l",16,40,102,40],["c",106,40,4],["l",100,40,84,40,"a"],["t",86,32,"T"],["t",108,32,"m"],["t",54,58,"ℓ"],["p","M26 64 Q44 40 26 22","a"],["t",8,18,"ω"],["c",16,120,2,"F"],["l",16,120,102,120],["c",106,120,4],["l",100,120,84,120,"a"],["t",86,112,"T"],["t",108,112,"m"],["t",54,138,"ℓ"],["p","M26 144 Q44 120 26 102","a"],["t",8,98,"2ω"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q004","pn":4,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Force and change of momentum","q":"A football player is moving southward and suddenly turns eastward with the same speed to avoid an opponent. The force that acts on the player while turning is :","o":["along south-west","along eastward","along northward","along north-east"],"c":3,"s":"Take east as î and north as ĵ.\nFinal momentum Pf = mu î. Initial momentum Pi = mu(−ĵ).\nChange in momentum ΔP = Pf − Pi = mu î + mu ĵ = mu(î + ĵ).\nForce F = ΔP/Δt has the same direction as the change in momentum.\nSo the force acts along north-east.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":170,"alt":"Compass axes: ĵ north (up), î east (right), west left, south down.","d":[["l",100,85,100,18,"a"],["l",100,85,100,152,"a"],["l",100,85,22,85,"a"],["l",100,85,178,85,"a"],["t",90,22,"ĵ","e"],["t",106,22,"N"],["t",186,89,"E"],["t",8,89,"W"],["t",184,78,"î"],["t",106,164,"S"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q046","pn":46,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Friction and pseudo force","q":"Calculate the maximum acceleration of a moving car so that a body lying on the floor of the car remains stationary, The coefficient of static friction between the body and the floor is 0.15 (g = 10 ms⁻²)","o":["50 ms⁻²","1.2 ms⁻²","150 ms⁻²","1.5 ms⁻²"],"c":3,"s":"In the car's frame the body is at rest, so the pseudo force m·a_max is balanced by limiting friction.\nm·a_max = μ_s·m·g.\na_max = μ_s g = 0.15 × 10 = 1.5 m/s².","sfig":{"w":280,"h":190,"alt":"Block on the floor of a car that accelerates to the right with a_max; free-body diagram of the block: m·a_max to the left and friction f_r to the right.","d":[["l",10,60,200,60],["r",35,30,90,24],["t",80,46,"car","m"],["r",70,20,12,10],["t",90,18,"block"],["c",55,66,6,""],["c",95,66,6,""],["l",135,40,185,40,"a"],["r",70,115,70,60],["l",140,145,200,145,"a"],["t",191,44,"a"],["t",198,47,"max","s"],["l",112,145,78,145,"a"],["l",140,145,200,145,"a"],["t",206,141,"f"],["t",211,144,"r","s"],["t",20,141,"ma"],["t",36,144,"max","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q007","pn":7,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Linear momentum","q":"The distance covered by a body of mass 5 g having linear momentum 0.3 kg m/s in 5 s is:","o":["0.3 m","300 m","30 m","3 m"],"c":1,"s":"Momentum p = mv, with m = 5 g = 5/1000 kg.\n0.3 = (5/1000) × v, so v = 60 m/s.\nDistance in 5 s = 60 × 5 = 300 m."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q031","pn":31,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Contact force on incline","q":"In the diagram shown, the normal reaction force between 2 kg and 1 kg is (Consider the surface, to be smooth): Given g = 10 ms⁻²","o":["10 N","25 N","39 N","6 N"],"c":1,"s":"Take all three blocks together. Net force up the incline = 60 − 18 − (3+2+1)g sin30° = 60 − 18 − 30 = 12 N.\nAcceleration a = 12/6 = 2 m/s² up the incline.\nFor the 1 kg block: N − 18 − 1×10×sin30° = 1×2.\nN − 23 = 2, so N = 25 N.","fig":{"w":350,"h":200,"alt":"A smooth incline of angle 30 degrees with blocks of 3 kg, 2 kg and 1 kg in contact; a 60 N force F1 pushes the 3 kg block up the slope and an 18 N force F2 pushes the 1 kg block down the slope.","d":[["l",20,185,330,185],["l",64,185,280.5063509461097,60,""],["p","M133.3 145L183.5 116L171.5 95.2L121.3 124.2Z"],["p","M183.5 116L223.3 93L211.3 72.2L171.5 95.2Z"],["p","M223.3 93L256.3 74L244.3 53.2L211.3 72.2Z"],["t",132.89676901250382,103.65321248268239,"3 kg","s"],["t",177.93009000929464,77.65321248268239,"2 kg","s"],["t",214.30315696824107,56.6532124826824,"1 kg","s"],["l",92.64101615137756,154.60769515458674,127.28203230275511,134.60769515458674,"a"],["t",30.641016151377556,157.60769515458674,"F₁ = 60 N","s"],["l",284.89865579152297,43.607695154586736,250.25763964014538,63.607695154586736,"a"],["t",292.89865579152297,39.607695154586736,"F₂ = 18 N","s"],["p","M98 185A34 34 0 0 0 93.4 168"],["t",106,181,"θ = 30°","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2021-q046","pn":46,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Impulse","q":"A ball of mass 0.15 kg is dropped from a height 10 m, strikes the ground and rebounds to the same height. The magnitude of impulse imparted to the ball is (g = 10 m/s²) nearly","o":["1.4 kg m/s","0 kg m/s","4.2 kg m/s","2.1 kg m/s"],"c":2,"s":"Speed just before and just after hitting the ground: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 10) = 10√2 ≈ 14.1 m/s (it rebounds to the same height).\nThe direction reverses, so change in momentum = m(v – (–v)) = 2mv.\nImpulse = 2 × 0.15 × 14.1 ≈ 4.2 kg m/s."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q093","pn":93,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Atwood machine","q":"Two bodies of mass 4 kg and 6 kg are tied to the ends of a massless string. The string passes over a pulley which is frictionless (see figure). The acceleration of the system in terms of acceleration due to gravity (g) is:","o":["g/2","g/5","g/10","g"],"c":1,"s":"The heavier mass (6 kg) moves down.\na = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂).\n= (6 − 4)g/(6 + 4) = 2g/10 = g/5.","fig":{"w":260,"h":180,"alt":"Frictionless pulley with a string; 4 kg block hangs on the left, 6 kg block hangs lower on the right","d":[["c",130,36,22],["c",130,36,1.5,"F"],["l",108,36,108,100],["l",152,36,152,126],["r",98,100,20,22],["r",142,126,20,22],["t",56,116,"4 kg"],["t",172,146,"6 kg"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2019-q093","pn":93,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Equilibrium of forces","q":"A particle moving with velocity V is acted by three forces shown by the vector triangle PQR. The velocity of the particle will :","o":["remain constant","change according to the smallest force QR","increase","decrease"],"c":0,"s":"The three force vectors form a closed triangle taken head to tail.\nSo their vector sum is zero and the net force is zero.\nWith no net force there is no acceleration, so the velocity stays the same.","fig":{"w":240,"h":190,"alt":"Right-angled triangle PQR with the right angle at R; the three force vectors RP, PQ and QR run round the triangle head to tail.","d":[["l",40,150,40,26,"a"],["l",40,26,190,150,"a"],["l",190,150,44,150,"a"],["t",35,22,"P","e"],["t",33,167,"R","e"],["t",197,167,"Q"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2019-q111","pn":111,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Vertical circular motion","q":"A mass m is attached to a thin wire and whirled in a vertical circle. The wire is most likely to break when :","o":["the mass is at the lowest point","inclined at an angle of 60° from vertical","the mass is at the highest point","the wire is horizontal"],"c":0,"s":"At the lowest point T − mg = mv²/r, so T = mg + mv²/r.\nThe speed is also greatest there.\nSo the tension is maximum at the lowest point and the wire breaks there."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q113","pn":113,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Circular motion and friction","q":"A block of mass 10 kg is in contact against the inner wall of a hollow cylindrical drum of radius 1 m. The coefficient of friction between the block and the inner wall of the cylinder is 0.1. The minimum angular velocity needed for the cylinder to keep the block stationary when the cylinder is vertical and rotating about its axis, will be : (g = 10 m/s²)","o":["10 rad/s","10π rad/s","√10 rad/s","10/(2π) rad/s"],"c":0,"s":"The wall pushes the block inward with N = mrω².\nFriction f = μN must balance weight: μmrω² = mg.\nω² = g/(μr) = 10/(0.1×1) = 100.\nω = 10 rad/s.","sfig":{"w":215,"h":230,"alt":"Vertical hollow cylinder with a block on its inner wall; friction f drawn upward and weight mg drawn downward on the block.","d":[["l",27,23.4,27,202.5],["l",180,23.4,180,202.5],["p","M27 23.4 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 180 23.4 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 27 23.4"],["p","M27 202.5 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 180 202.5 A76.5 13.5 0 1 0 27 202.5"],["p","M27 114.8 A69.8 13.5 0 1 0 166.6 114.8 A69.8 13.5 0 1 0 27 114.8","d"],["r",166.5,99,12.6,29.3],["l",173.3,96.8,173.3,64.4,"a"],["l",173.3,130.5,173.3,162,"a"],["t",185.4,74.3,"f","s"],["t",185.4,159.8,"mg","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2019-q115","pn":115,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Motion on an inclined plane","q":"When an object is shot from the bottom of a long smooth inclined plane kept at an angle 60° with horizontal, it can travel a distance x₁ along the plane. But when the inclination is decreased to 30° and the same object is shot with the same velocity, it can travel x₂ distance. Then x₁ : x₂ will be :","o":["1 : √3","1 : 2√3","1 : √2","√2 : 1"],"c":0,"s":"Deceleration along the plane is g sinθ.\n0 = u² − 2g sinθ·x, so x = u²/(2g sinθ), so x ∝ 1/sinθ.\nx₁/x₂ = sin30°/sin60° = (1/2)/(√3/2) = 1/√3.\nSo x₁ : x₂ = 1 : √3.","sfig":{"w":230,"h":170,"alt":"Right-angled inclined plane with a block at the bottom of the slope moving up it with velocity u.","d":[["p","M52.5 156 L202.5 12.5 L202.5 156 Z"],["p","M52.5 154 L31 126 L55 104 L80 131 Z"],["l",61,121.5,93,92.5,"a"],["t",100,84,"u","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2018-q015","pn":15,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Friction","q":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect ?","o":["Rolling friction is smaller than sliding friction","Limiting value of static friction is directly proportional to normal reaction","Frictional force opposes the relative motion","Coefficient of sliding friction has dimensions of length"],"c":3,"s":"Friction f = μN, so μ = f/N.\nBoth f and N are forces, so their dimensions cancel.\nμ is dimensionless, so statement (4) is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q028","pn":28,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Laws of Motion","st":"Pseudo force on an inclined wedge","q":"A block of mass m is placed on a smooth inclined wedge ABC of inclination θ as shown in the figure. The wedge is given an acceleration 'a' towards the right. The relation between a and θ for the block to remain stationary on the wedge is","o":["a = (g)/(cosecθ)","a = (g)/(sinθ)","a = g cosθ","a = g tanθ"],"c":3,"s":"In the wedge's frame the block feels a pseudo force ma to the left.\nAlong the incline: mg sinθ (down the slope) is balanced by ma cosθ (up the slope).\nmg sinθ = ma cosθ, so a = g tanθ.","fig":{"w":310,"h":190,"alt":"Wedge ABC with vertical side AC, base CB and angle theta at B; a block of mass m rests on the slope and the wedge accelerates to the right with acceleration a.","d":[["l",70,150,250,150,""],["l",100,30,100,150,""],["l",100,30,250,150,""],["l",104,150,168.9,85.1,""],["l",113,150,173.9,89.1,""],["l",122,150,178.9,93.1,""],["l",131,150,183.9,97.1,""],["l",140,150,188.9,101.1,""],["l",149,150,193.9,105.1,""],["l",158,150,198.9,109.1,""],["l",167,150,203.9,113.1,""],["l",176,150,208.9,117.1,""],["l",185,150,213.9,121.1,""],["l",194,150,218.9,125.1,""],["l",248,150,248.9,149.1,""],["l",100,142,162.2,79.8,""],["l",100,134,157.8,76.2,""],["l",100,126,153.3,72.7,""],["l",100,118,148.9,69.1,""],["l",100,110,144.4,65.6,""],["l",100,102,140,62,""],["l",100,94,135.6,58.4,""],["l",100,86,131.1,54.9,""],["l",100,78,126.7,51.3,""],["l",100,70,122.2,47.8,""],["l",100,62,117.8,44.2,""],["l",100,54,113.3,40.7,""],["l",100,46,108.9,37.1,""],["l",100,38,104.4,33.6,""],["p","M171.9 87.5L200 110L213.8 92.8L185.6 70.3Z","f"],["t",205.9,68.8,"m",""],["l",219,125,268,125,"a"],["t",274,129,"a",""],["p","M220 150A30 30 0 0 1 226.6 131.2",""],["t",220,143,"θ","s"],["t",94,30,"A","e"],["t",100,168,"C","m"],["t",250,168,"B","m"]]},"sfig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"Free-body diagram of the block on the wedge: N perpendicular to the slope, mg sinθ down the slope, ma cosθ up the slope, and mg cosθ and ma sinθ into the slope.","d":[["l",70,170,270,170,""],["l",100,40,100,170,""],["l",100,40,270,170,""],["p","M166.7 91L192.1 110.5L205.5 93L180.1 73.6Z",""],["l",186.7,91.2,214.1,55.5,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,147,60.8,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,226.4,121.6,"a"],["l",186.7,91.2,161.2,124.6,"a"],["t",220.1,53.5,"N",""],["t",139,48.8,"ma cosθ",""],["t",234.4,131.6,"mg sinθ",""],["t",153.2,122.6,"ma sinθ","e"],["t",165.2,140.6,"mg cosθ","e"],["l",262,52,305,52,"a"],["t",311,56,"a",""],["p","M240 170A30 30 0 0 1 246.2 151.8",""],["t",241,166,"θ","s"],["t",100,188,"C","m"],["t",270,188,"B","m"]]}}
